rd with a hand containing several isolated single cards (3, 6, 10, Q, A), this is an ideal opportunity to play one of your lower singles. You possess a strong top end (222, A) and some pairs (44, 77, KK), but your victory depends on clearing the low "junk" singles. Passing here would be a mistake as it leaves you stuck with the 10. Playing the Q, A, or breaking the KK pair or 222 triplet would be inefficient use of rank. The 10 is the lowest card you have that legally beats the 7, preserving your higher cards for later control.\n\nReason: You have a single 10 which is your lowest card capable of beating the ♣7. It is crucial to shed mid-range singles when the opportunity arises to declutter your hand without wasting high-value cards like the Ace or 2s.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♣10"]}'}
